  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Northbrook</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Northbrook</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Postwar Mortar Joint Crumbling</h3>
          <p>50–70 years of Cook County freeze-thaw cycling has crumbled original Type S or N mortar in exposed joints. Water infiltration worsens each winter — repointing now prevents the damage that makes future repairs far more costly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Previous Repair Mismatch (Portland over Lime)</h3>
          <p>Hard Portland cement over softer original mortar traps moisture at the brick interface, causing face spalling. Removing incompatible prior repair material is now the most common first step on Northbrook jobs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Chimney Cap and Crown Failure on 1960s Homes</h3>
          <p>60 years of freeze-thaw cycling cracks original concrete crowns, letting water enter the flue. Crown failure is the leading cause of interior chimney damage — early repair prevents full reconstruction.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Grade-Level Mortar Deterioration on Flat Lots</h3>
          <p>Flat lots concentrate ground moisture at the foundation-to-brick interface. North and west grade-level joints fail first — the primary entry point for water damaging foundation walls and interior framing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

